## Sync State

Optional. Bridge keeps the game world on the host and copies it to the other players. Every player enters the room with the `syncState` option: `true`, or `{ updatesPerSecond: 5 }` for fewer snapshots a second (from 1 to 10, 10 by default). The host sets its world whenever it changes, and up to `updatesPerSecond` times a second Bridge sends the latest one to the other players: they get it with the sync state received event. The server keeps the last world: when the host leaves or drops, the new host gets it with the same event and the game goes on from the same point. The world is passed in the same format as [message](#send-messages) data.

Without `syncState` the game sends its state with messages itself, and a new host has only what its own device knows.

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Plain JS" %}

```javascript
let world = { score: 0, ball: { x: 0, y: 0 } }

// The starting world, set before entering the room
bridge.multiplayer.setSyncState(world)

// Every player enters the room with syncState
bridge.multiplayer.createRoom({ syncState: { updatesPerSecond: 5 } })
// bridge.multiplayer.joinRoom(roomId, { syncState: true })
// bridge.multiplayer.joinOrCreateRoom({ maxPlayers: 2, syncState: true })

// The other players get the host's world, a new host gets the last one
bridge.multiplayer.on(bridge.EVENT_NAME.MULTIPLAYER_SYNC_STATE_RECEIVED, state => {
    world = state
})

// Players send their actions to the host, the host changes the world
bridge.multiplayer.on(bridge.EVENT_NAME.MULTIPLAYER_MESSAGE_RECEIVED, message => {
    if (bridge.multiplayer.isHost && message.type === 'move') {
        world.ball = message.data
        bridge.multiplayer.setSyncState(world)
    }
})

bridge.multiplayer.syncState // the last world set or received
```

Instead of `setSyncState()` and the event, `syncState` can take callbacks: Bridge takes the world from `getState()` on the host and passes received worlds to `applyState()`. Both must be synchronous and may run before the entry promise resolves; do not check `isHost` in `getState()`, the first snapshot is taken before the host role is confirmed.

```javascript
bridge.multiplayer.createRoom({
    syncState: {
        getState: () => world,
        applyState: state => { world = state },
        updatesPerSecond: 5,
    },
})
```

{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Unity" %}

```csharp
var world = new Dictionary<string, object> { { "score", 0 }, { "ballX", 0 }, { "ballY", 0 } };

// The starting world, set before entering the room
Bridge.multiplayer.SetSyncState(world);

// The other players get the host's world, a new host gets the last one
Bridge.multiplayer.syncStateReceived += state =>
{
    world = state;
    var score = Convert.ToInt32(world["score"]);
};

// Every player enters the room with syncState: true, or a dictionary with updatesPerSecond
var options = new Dictionary<string, object>
{
    { "syncState", new Dictionary<string, object> { { "updatesPerSecond", 5 } } },
};

Bridge.multiplayer.CreateRoom(options);
// Bridge.multiplayer.JoinRoom(roomId, options);
// Bridge.multiplayer.JoinOrCreateRoom(new Dictionary<string, object> { { "maxPlayers", 2 }, { "syncState", true } });

// Players send their actions to the host, the host changes the world
Bridge.multiplayer.messageReceived += message =>
{
    if (Bridge.multiplayer.isHost && message.type == "move")
    {
        world["ballX"] = message.data["x"];
        world["ballY"] = message.data["y"];
        Bridge.multiplayer.SetSyncState(world);
    }
};

Bridge.multiplayer.syncState // the last world set or received
```

The world is a flat dictionary, like message data: nested values arrive as JSON strings.
{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Construct 3" %}
