* Every player enters the room with `syncState`, on every room entry. When a player without it becomes the host, its empty world replaces the game.
* Change the world only on the host and set it again after every change: Bridge keeps a copy. The other players send their actions to the host with [messages](#send-messages); the next snapshot overwrites their own changes.
* Set the starting world and subscribe to the event before entering the room: the first snapshot is taken right after entering, and a new host may get the world before the entry completes. The world is cleared when the player leaves the room.
* Each snapshot is the whole world, not a change. Snapshots and messages share one limit of 64 KB a second per player: when the world does not fit, Bridge sends snapshots less often and skips the ones in between. Keep the world small; fast-paced games should send their updates with messages.
* A world that is not a JSON object is not set, and a world larger than 64 KB stops synchronization for this player: it can no longer be the host. Bridge logs a warning.

## Errors

Failed requests report a code: `error.code` in Plain JS and Cocos Creator, `lastError` in Unity, `last_error` in Godot, the failure data in GameMaker and Defold, `MultiplayerLastErrorCode` in Construct 3 and `MultiplayerLastError()` in GDevelop.

| Code                            | Meaning                                                                                           |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `MULTIPLAYER_NOT_SUPPORTED`     | No public token, or the platform does not support multiplayer.                                    |
| `MULTIPLAYER_ALREADY_IN_ROOM`   | The player is already in a room or entering one.                                                  |
| `MULTIPLAYER_NOT_IN_ROOM`       | The call needs a room.                                                                            |
| `MULTIPLAYER_ROOM_NOT_FOUND`    | No room with this code, or the room has closed.                                                   |
| `MULTIPLAYER_ROOM_FULL`         | The room has no free seat.                                                                        |
| `MULTIPLAYER_ROOM_CLOSED`       | The room does not accept new players.                                                             |
| `MULTIPLAYER_INVALID_REQUEST`   | The server refused the call arguments, e.g. a wrong filter or too large properties: fix the call. |
| `MULTIPLAYER_CONNECTION_FAILED` | The server could not be reached.                                                                  |

Do not retry `joinRoom` in a loop: after `MULTIPLAYER_ROOM_NOT_FOUND`, `MULTIPLAYER_ROOM_FULL` or `MULTIPLAYER_ROOM_CLOSED`, a repeated join to the same room gets the same error at once, without a request, for 1 second, and the wait doubles with each failure in a row up to 30 seconds. Offer the player another room or `joinOrCreateRoom` instead.